      <workList xml:id="idm56">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e183180">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_967c28d2">A4:200</identifier>
            <title xml:id="_13" xml:lang="it">Fantasie overo dialoghi per musica a due cembali</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_321f04b6">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="_14">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_15_0">
               <p xml:id="idm64">The fantasia for two harpsichords which once belonged to <persName xml:id="idm65" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Heinrich Wilhelm von Gerstenberg</persName>, one of Scheibe’s close acquaintances, seems to have been lost. The work which is set up as a dialogue between a happy youngster and capricious old man, is referred to in an auction catalogue of Gerstenberg’s estate which contained a collection of books and music sold in 1824. Gerstenberg was an able keyboard player, and it is likely that he got the work from Scheibe. The idea of setting a dialogue in music was also taken up by <persName xml:id="idm66" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach</persName> in a trio for two violins and continuo, and Gerstenberg set two sets of lyrics to one of Bach’s Fantasias (‘<persName xml:id="idm67" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Socrates</persName> drinking the poison cup’ and ‘<persName xml:id="idm68" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Hamlet</persName>’s monologue’). It is in this context that the present dialogue should be seen.</p>
            </history>
